- Analyze performance implications on 64-bit architectures with compiler optimizations.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests 128-bit reasoning, non-branching logic, and careful boundary handling across hi/lo halves. The value is a power of two when exactly one bit in the 128-bit space is set.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit value split into hi/lo; treat as two 64-bit lanes.
- Power-of-two check: a nonzero 64-bit word is a power of two if (x & (x-1)) == 0.
- Branchless composition: the single 1 must reside either in lo (and hi == 0) or in hi (and lo == 0).

## Code Example
```javascript
// Implementation sketch in C-like syntax (language-agnostic semantics)
int isPowerOfTwo128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  unsigned int loCase = (lo != 0) && ((lo & (lo - 1)) == 0) && (hi == 0);
  unsigned int hiCase = (hi != 0) && ((hi & (hi - 1)) == 0) && (lo == 0);
  return (loCase || hiCase) ? 1 : 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to an embedded environment with no 64-bit ops available?
- How would you verify correctness across all 128-bit values efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to implement conditional data movement without branching. It demonstrates how to construct a full-word mask from a single flag and apply it consistently across 128-bit halves.

## Key Concepts
- **Branchless conditioning via masks:** Using arithmetic to convert a boolean into an all-0s or all-1s mask
- **128-bit representation:** A 128-bit value split into hi/lo 64-bit halves
- **Portable unsigned arithmetic:** Safe, well-defined behavior across platforms

## Code Example
```c
uint128 swapHalvesIf128(uint128 x, unsigned c) {
    unsigned m = -(unsigned)(c != 0);
    uint64_t hi = (x.hi & ~m) | (x.lo & m);
    uint64_t lo = (x.lo & ~m) | (x.hi & m);
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about scanning a 128-bit value with fixed, branch-reduced steps and no loops. It exercises bitwise practice and edge-case handling.

## Key Concepts
- Fixed-width binary search over bit positions
- Handling x==0 gracefully
- Portability across 128-bit types

## Code Example
```c
// Conceptual outline (branch-free version would use masks)
int msbIndex128(uint128 x) {
    if (x == 0) return -1; // illustrative; actual solution avoids branches
    int idx = 0;
    if (x >> 64) { x >>= 64; idx += 64; }
    if (x >> 32) { x >>= 32; idx += 32; }
    if (x >> 16) { x >>= 16; idx += 16; }
    if (x >> 8)  { x >>= 8;  idx += 8; }
    if (x >> 4)  { x >>= 4;  idx += 4; }
    if (x >> 2)  { x >>= 2;  idx += 2; }
    if (x >> 1)  { idx += 1; }
    return idx;
}
```

