## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to unpack bit-packed fields without branches—a common low-level task in networking, codecs, and embedded systems.

## Key Concepts

- **Bitwise masks and shifts** extract subfields from packed integers efficiently
- **No branches** ensures constant-time execution, critical for performance-sensitive code
- **Endianness** is irrelevant when masking at exact bit positions

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
    uint8_t  version;
    uint8_t  type;
    uint16_t length;
    uint8_t  flags;
} Header;

Header extractHeaderFields(uint32_t w) {
    Header h;
    h.version = (w >> 28) & 0xF;  // Bits 31-28
    h.type    = (w >> 24) & 0xF;  // Bits 27-24
    h.length  = (w >> 8)  & 0xFFFF; // Bits 23-8
    h.flags   = w & 0xFF;          // Bits 7-0
    return h;
}
```

Each shift moves the target field to the least significant position, then the mask isolates the relevant bits. This approach is branchless, deterministic, and executes in constant time.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of advanced bit-twiddling for permutation of set bits. Evaluates ability to craft constant-time logic without loops and to reason about edge cases of the last permutation.

## Key Concepts

- Next higher permutation with fixed popcount
- Edge-case handling (max permutation -> 0)
- Pure bitwise operations and shifts; no loops or branches
- Efficient 64-bit handling and overflow behavior

## Code Example

```javascript
uint64_t nextBitPermutation64(uint64_t x) {
  uint64_t smallest = x & -x;
  uint64_t ripple = x + smallest;
  uint64_t ones = ripple & -ripple;
  uint64_t next = ripple | (((ones ^ ripple) >> 2) / smallest);
  return next;
}
```

## Examples

- `nextBitPermutation64(0b0011)` returns `0b0101` (next permutation with 2 set bits)
- `nextBitPermutation64(0b1100)` returns `0b10000` (next permutation with 2 set bits)
- `nextBitPermutation64(0b111000)` returns `0b1000011` (next permutation with 3 set bits)

## Why This Is Asked
This tests advanced bit manipulation skills, requiring multi-word arithmetic without 128-bit native support. It demonstrates branchless programming techniques and careful handling of shift operations.

## Key Concepts
- Represent 128-bit values as two 64-bit words (hi, lo)
- Decompose k into t = k mod 64 (intra-word shift) and b = k div 64 (cross-word flag)
- Branchless selection using bitwise operations and masks
- Handle edge cases like k=0 and k=64 without conditionals

## Code Example
```c
void rotate_left_128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, unsigned k, 
                    uint64_t* out_hi, uint64_t* out_lo) {
    uint64_t t = k & 63;
    uint64_t b = (k >> 6) & 1;

    uint64_t hiA = (hi << t) | (lo >> (64 - t));
    uint64_t loA = (lo << t) | (hi >> (64 - t));
    uint64_t hiB = (lo << t) | (hi >> (64 - t));
    uint64_t loB = (hi << t) | (lo >> (64 - t));

    *out_hi = hiA ^ ((hiA ^ hiB) & -b);
    *out_lo = loA ^ ((loA ^ loB) & -b);
}
```

## Examples
- k=1, hi=0, lo=1 → out_hi=1, out_lo=0
- k=64, hi=1, lo=0 → out_hi=0, out_lo=1

## Why This Is Asked
Real-world bitfield extraction often needs to pull a contiguous block from a 128-bit value into the least-significant bits for parsing, with no branching for performance.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit arithmetic using two 64-bit halves
- Bit masking and shifting across a 128-bit boundary
- Pure bitwise manipulations (no loops)

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 extractBitBlock128(uint128 x, unsigned start, unsigned width) {
  // Build mask in 128-bit form: width ones at LSBs
  uint128 M = {0,0};
  if (width == 128) { M.hi = ~0ULL; M.lo = ~0ULL; }
  else if (width < 64) { M.hi = 0; M.lo = (width==0)?0:((1ULL<<width)-1); }
  else { M.lo = ~0ULL; M.hi = (width-64==0)?0:((1ULL<<(width-64))-1); }
  // Shift right by start and apply mask
  // Implement 128-bit shift and AND here respecting the architecture
  uint128 y = {0,0};
  // (Assume helper shifts across hi/lo are available)
  y = shrl128(x, start);
  return and128(y, M);
}
```

